":0,"title":["Gaussian-Based Periodic Grand Canonical Density Functional Theory with Implicit Solvation for Computational Electrochemistry"],"prefix":"10.1021","member":"316","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,11]],"date-time":"2025-11-11T09:08:37Z","timestamp":1762852117000},"score":51.696033,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/pubs.acs.org\/doi\/suppl\/10.1021\/acs.jctc.5c01403\/suppl_file\/ct5c01403_si_001.pdf"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[null]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1021\/acs.jctc.5c01403.s001","relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.1021\/acs.jctc.5c01403","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,14]],"date-time":"2026-05-14T10:07:30Z","timestamp":1778753250721,"version":"3.51.4"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,6]]},"reference-count":0,"publisher":"American Chemical Society (ACS)","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,6]],"date-time":"2025-03-06T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1741219200000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by-nc-nd\/4.0\/"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>We present an implementation of analytical nuclear gradient and hessian for grand-canonical density-functional theory (GC-DFT) in the Kohn-Sham formulation. Despite the fact that the average occupation numbers of orbitals change with the geometric structures of molecules, it is shown that evaluation of nuclear gradient of GC-DFT can be formulated in a way similar to that of nuclear gradient of micro-canonical (\u03bcC) DFT, without the need for the knowledge of the changes in the occupation numbers. On the other hand, in contrast to that of \u03bcC-DFT, the nuclear hessian of GC-DFT encompasses two components concerning both the fixed and variable occupation numbers, as a result of the chain rule of differentiation. We have developed two techniques, namely the non-idempotent (NI) coupled-perturbed self-consistent field (CPSCF) and the occupation-gradient (OG) CPSCF for those two components, respectively. The analytical nuclear derivatives are verified via comparison with numerical results given by the finite-difference method.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.26434\/chemrxiv-2023-gp81w-v4","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,6]],"date-time":"2025-03-06T05:39:06Z","timestamp":1741239546000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Navigating the Electronic Grand Potential Surface with Analytical Nuclear Derivatives of Grand-Canonical Density-Functional Theory"],"prefix":"10.26434","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9944-1997","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yichi","family":"ZHANG","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Chemistry, The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Kowloon, Hong Kong, China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ka Lok","family":"Chan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Chemistry, The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Kowloon, Hong Kong, China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Fu Kit","family":"Sheong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Chemistry, The Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Kowloon, Hong Kong, China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"316","link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/chemrxiv.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.26434\/chemrxiv-2023-gp81w-v4","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,14]],"date-time":"2026-05-14T09:08:04Z","timestamp":1778749684000},"score":43.44237,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/chemrxiv.org\/doi\/full\/10.26434\/chemrxiv-2023-gp81w-v4"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,6]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.26434\/chemrxiv-2023-gp81w-v4","relation":{"is-preprint-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.1021\/acs.jctc.5c00575","asserted-by":"subject"}]},"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,6]]},"subtype":"preprint"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,14]],"date-time":"2026-05-14T10:26:30Z","timestamp":1778754390774,"version":"3.51.4"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,10]]},"reference-count":0,"publisher":"American Chemical Society (ACS)","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,10]],"date-time":"2025-04-10T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1744243200000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by-nc-nd\/4.0\/"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>We present an implementation of analytical nuclear gradient and hessian for grand-canonical density-functional theory (GC-DFT) in the Kohn-Sham formulation. Despite the fact that the average occupation numbers of orbitals change with the geometric structures of molecules, it is shown that evaluation of nuclear gradient of GC-DFT can be formulated in a way similar to that of nuclear gradient of micro-canonical (\u03bcC) DFT, without the need for the knowledge of the changes in the occupation numbers. On the other hand, in contrast to that of \u03bcC-DFT, the nuclear hessian of GC-DFT encompasses two components concerning both the fixed and variable occupation numbers, as a result of the chain rule of differentiation. We have developed three techniques, namely the non-idempotent (NI) coupled-perturbed self-consistent field (CPSCF), the occupation-gradient (OG) CPSCF and the occupation-fluctuation (OF) CPSCF for those two components. The analytical nuclear derivatives are verified via comparison with numerical results given by the finite-difference method. The application of the nuclear derivatives of GC-DFT is demonstrated with an example.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.26434\/chemrxiv-2023-gp81w-v5","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,10]],"date-time":"2025-04-10T07:35:01Z","timestamp":1744270501000},"source":"Crossref",